from .common import *
from .lg import S


def theory(B):
    B.h2("11.6  Electronic structure of nitrenes, nitrenoids and nitrenium ions")
    B.ul(["<b>Valence electron count.</b> N has five valence electrons. In R–N: one is in the R–N σ bond and four are non-bonding: a total of six electrons around N (sextet), two of them in the σ-bonding pair. The four non-bonding electrons occupy an s-like (sp) orbital and two degenerate p orbitals (p<sub>x</sub>, p<sub>y</sub>) perpendicular to the R–N axis. Three electronic states arise from placing two electrons in the two p orbitals: the ground-state triplet ³Σ<sup>−</sup> (↑ in p<sub>x</sub>, ↑ in p<sub>y</sub>), and two closed-shell-like singlets (¹Δ, ¹Σ<sup>+</sup>). For NH: triplet X ³Σ<sup>−</sup>, singlet a ¹Δ at ≈ 36 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>, b ¹Σ<sup>+</sup> at ≈ 60.",
          "<b>Why the gap is larger than in carbenes.</b> The two p orbitals of R–N: are exactly degenerate (the R–N axis is a C<sub>∞</sub> axis for a monovalent N), so Hund’s rule places the electrons in separate orbitals with parallel spin; in a bent carbene the σ and p orbitals differ in energy and the singlet is competitive. Nitrene triplets are ground states for alkyl, aryl and NH; singlets are ground states only when strongly stabilised (e.g. N-amino-nitrenes R<sub>2</sub>N–N: ‘aminonitrenes’, and some acyl/imino nitrenes by lone-pair donation).",
          "<b>Substituent effects.</b> π-Donating R groups (NR<sub>2</sub>, OR, X) donate to an empty p orbital of the singlet and so reduce the gap: aminonitrenes (R<sub>2</sub>N–N:) are singlets; the nitrene of the acyl type RC(=O)–N: is a triplet in the gas phase but the singlet is lower than expected because C=O π overlaps one p orbital. Aryl nitrenes: triplet ground state by 14–18 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> for phenylnitrene (the unpaired electron is delocalised into the ring, giving a strong ESR signal of the triplet in a matrix). Alkyl nitrenes: triplet ground state, but a singlet alkylnitrene undergoes 1,2-H shift to an imine so fast (k &gt; 10<sup>10</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>) that it is rarely seen.",
          "<b>Nitrenoids.</b> Many ‘nitrene’ reactions are really transfers from metal-bound or N–X nitrogen with no free R–N:. Examples: iminoiodinanes PhI=NTs, chloramine-T and bromamine-T with Cu, Rh, Fe or Mn catalysts (metal–imido/nitrenoid intermediates, Du Bois C–H amination, Sharpless aminohydroxylation with Os); N-chloroamines + base; azides + metal. The metal-bound nitrenoid is more selective than a free nitrene and can be rendered enantioselective.",
          "<b>Nitrenium ions.</b> R<sub>2</sub>N<sup>+</sup> (six electrons, positive charge, isoelectronic with a carbene) are singlets (or triplets for diarylnitrenium) formed by heterolysis of N–X (N-chloroamines + Ag<sup>+</sup>), protonation of N-hydroxy-/N-acyloxyarylamines (the metabolic activation of aromatic amine carcinogens: ArN(OH)Ac → ArN<sup>+</sup>Ac, which alkylates DNA at guanine C-8) and in Bamberger rearrangement of N-phenylhydroxylamine in aqueous acid (PhNHOH + H<sup>+</sup> → PhNH<sup>+</sup> → p-aminophenol by attack of water at the para position). Nitrenium ions are delocalised (iminium–carbocation resonance: Ar–N<sup>+</sup>R ↔ cyclohexadienyl-type cation with positive charge at ortho and para carbons), which is why nucleophiles attack the ring."])
    B.h2("11.7  Generation of nitrenes: a complete catalogue")
    B.table(["Method", "Precursor and conditions", "Notes"],
        [["Alkyl/aryl azides", "R–N<sub>3</sub>, hν (254–300 nm) or Δ (&gt; 150 °C; aryl azides 100–140 °C)", "the cleanest source; hazard: low-MW azides are shock-sensitive and explosive (rule of six: ≥ 6 carbons per azide); N<sub>2</sub> is the leaving group; initially singlet, relaxes to triplet in solution"],
         ["Acyl azides", "RCON<sub>3</sub>, Δ (60–100 °C) → isocyanate directly; hν → acylnitrene (partly)", "concerted Curtius; the same azide photolysed gives nitrene insertion products besides isocyanate"],
         ["Sulfonyl and phosphoryl azides", "ArSO<sub>2</sub>N<sub>3</sub>, hν or Δ; (RO)<sub>2</sub>P(O)N<sub>3</sub>", "sulfonylnitrenes insert into C–H, add to alkenes (aziridines) and to arenes; DPPA (diphenyl phosphoryl azide) for the Shioiri–Yamada one-pot Curtius"],
         ["Vinyl azides", "R<sub>2</sub>C=CR–N<sub>3</sub>, Δ or hν", "give 2H-azirines (Neber-type ring closure) via vinylnitrene or concerted loss of N<sub>2</sub>; azirines are versatile building blocks"],
         ["Azidoformates", "ROCON<sub>3</sub>, hν or Δ (or Rh<sub>2</sub>)", "carbalkoxynitrenes: aziridines and C–H insertion (carbamates of amines)"],
         ["N-Aminophthalimide / N-amino heterocycles", "Pb(OAc)<sub>4</sub> or PhI(OAc)<sub>2</sub> oxidation", "Atkinson: N-phthalimidonitrene (singlet) adds stereospecifically to alkenes: a high-yield aziridination"],
         ["α-Elimination of N-halo / N-sulfonyloxy", "R–NH–X + base; ArSO<sub>2</sub>O–NHR", "Hofmann (Br), Lossen (carboxylate), Tiemann (sulfonates); nitrenoid"],
         ["Deoxygenation of nitroarenes / nitroso", "ArNO<sub>2</sub> + P(OEt)<sub>3</sub>, Δ (Cadogan–Sundberg); ArNO + PPh<sub>3</sub>", "arylnitrene → carbazoles, indoles (Sundberg: 2-nitrostyrene), benzimidazoles, 2H-indazoles"],
         ["Iminoiodinanes + metals", "PhI=NTs/Cu(I)Cl or Rh<sub>2</sub>(OAc)<sub>4</sub>, Ru-porphyrin", "metallonitrene transfer: aziridination of alkenes (Evans), C–H amination (Du Bois/Che: sulfamate esters + Rh<sub>2</sub>(esp)<sub>2</sub>/PhI(OAc)<sub>2</sub> → cyclic sulfamidates)"],
         ["Photolysis of isocyanates, azirines, tetrazoles", "RNCO → R–N: + CO; azirine ⇌ vinylnitrene", "less common"],
         ["Thermolysis of triazoles/tetrazoles", "5-substituted tetrazoles → imidoyl nitrene", "heterocyclic nitrene chemistry; ring-chain isomerism"]],
        widths=[22, 36, 42])
    B.h2("11.8  Reactions of nitrenes in detail")
    B.h3("(a) Insertion into C–H bonds (and into N–H, O–H, Si–H)")
    B.ul(["Singlet nitrenes insert in a single step with retention of configuration at the carbon (like singlet carbenes); triplet nitrenes abstract H and the resulting radical pair recombines (loss of configuration, mixtures).",
          "<b>Selectivity:</b> sulfonyl- and carbalkoxy-nitrenes (electrophilic) favour tertiary &gt; secondary &gt; primary C–H (≈ 1 : 7 : 30–100 for metal nitrenes), and activated C–H (benzylic, allylic, α to O) are the most reactive.",
          "<b>Intramolecular C–H amination</b> builds pyrrolidines (1,5-insertion through a six-membered TS) and indolines from azides: the basis of C–H functionalisation in total synthesis (Du Bois: carbamates give oxazolidinones; sulfamates give oxathiazinanes; each the key step of tetrodotoxin and manzacidin syntheses)."])
    B.h3("(b) Addition to C=C: aziridination, and why the alkene geometry tells the spin state")
    B.p("Singlet nitrenes add syn and stereospecifically: cis-alkene → cis-aziridine. Triplet nitrenes add stepwise to a 1,3-diradical that closes after intersystem crossing, so geometry is partly lost. With arenes, nitrenes give azepines (ring expansion), by aziridination of one arene bond (azanorcaradiene ⇌ azepine, the nitrogen analogue of the Buchner reaction).")
    B.h3("(c) Dimerisation, abstraction, and fragmentation")
    B.ul(["Two triplet nitrenes dimerise to the azo compound: 2 ArN: → ArN=NAr (azobenzene from phenylnitrene; the major product of triplet aryl nitrenes in the absence of traps).",
          "Triplet nitrenes abstract hydrogen from the solvent to give the aminyl radical R–NH•, then R–NH<sub>2</sub> (aniline from phenyl azide in cyclohexane).",
          "Alkyl nitrenes undergo a 1,2-H or 1,2-alkyl shift to an imine (R–CH<sub>2</sub>–N: → R–CH=NH), the nitrogen version of the carbene 1,2-H shift; acylnitrenes → isocyanates; vinyl nitrenes → azirines; azidoalkenes → nitriles by fragmentation; α-azido ketone → loss of N<sub>2</sub> gives α-imino ketone."])
    B.h2("11.9  The nitrogen-migration family: scope, mechanism, stereochemistry, applications")
    B.p("The four reactions below (and the related Beckmann, Stieglitz and Tiemann rearrangements) all convert a C–R bond into an N–R bond by a 1,2-shift to an electron-deficient nitrogen. In the <b>Hofmann–Curtius–Lossen–Schmidt</b> family the migrating terminus is the carbonyl carbon and the product is an isocyanate; the amine formed after hydrolysis has <b>one carbon fewer</b> (CO<sub>2</sub> lost). In the <b>Beckmann</b> and <b>Schmidt (ketone)</b> variants the product is an amide/lactam with the carbonyl carbon retained.")
    B.table(["Reaction", "Substrate", "Reagents", "Group lost from N", "Product", "Key features"],
        [["Hofmann", "RCONH<sub>2</sub>", "Br<sub>2</sub>/NaOH (NaOBr); or NaOCl; Pb(OAc)<sub>4</sub>; PhI(OCOCF<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>", "Br<sup>−</sup>", "RNH<sub>2</sub> (+ CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2−</sup>)", "4 NaOH + Br<sub>2</sub> per amide; isocyanate trapped by ROH gives carbamates (modified Hofmann: NBS/DBU/MeOH)"],
         ["Curtius", "RCON<sub>3</sub>", "Δ; or RCOOH + DPPA/Et<sub>3</sub>N (one pot)", "N<sub>2</sub>", "RNCO → RNH<sub>2</sub>, RNHCO<sub>2</sub>R′, RNHCONHR′", "mildest, no strong base; compatible with sensitive groups; Boc-protected amines with t-BuOH"],
         ["Lossen", "RCONHOCOR′ (O-acyl hydroxamate) or ArSO<sub>2</sub>ONHCOR", "base or Δ", "R′COO<sup>−</sup>", "RNH<sub>2</sub>", "hydroxamic acids from ester + NH<sub>2</sub>OH; useful when halogen oxidant is undesirable"],
         ["Schmidt (acid)", "RCOOH", "HN<sub>3</sub>, conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, Δ", "N<sub>2</sub>", "RNH<sub>2</sub>", "acylium + HN<sub>3</sub> → protonated acyl azide"],
         ["Schmidt (ketone)", "R<sub>2</sub>C=O", "HN<sub>3</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> (or TiCl<sub>4</sub>)", "N<sub>2</sub>", "amide RCONHR or lactam", "cyclohexanone → caprolactam (nylon-6); anti group to N<sub>2</sub> migrates"],
         ["Beckmann", "R<sub>2</sub>C=N–OH (oxime)", "H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, PCl<sub>5</sub>, TsCl/py, SOCl<sub>2</sub>, PPA", "H<sub>2</sub>O", "amide RCONHR′", "group <i>anti</i> to the leaving OH migrates (stereospecific); nitrilium ion; fragmentation when R is stabilised (abnormal Beckmann)"],
         ["Stieglitz", "R<sub>3</sub>C–NH–OH or N-chloroamine", "Ag<sup>+</sup>, PCl<sub>5</sub>", "H<sub>2</sub>O or Cl<sup>−</sup>", "imine R<sub>2</sub>C=NR", "carbon → nitrogen shift to nitrenium; trityl hydroxylamine"],
         ["Tiemann", "amidoxime RC(=NOH)NH<sub>2</sub> + ArSO<sub>2</sub>Cl", "base", "ArSO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>", "N-substituted cyanamide", "—"]],
        widths=[10, 14, 20, 12, 16, 28])
    B.ul(["<b>Evidence against a free acylnitrene in Hofmann/Curtius reactions in solution.</b> (i) Thermolysis of acyl azides gives isocyanate quantitatively with no insertion or addition products from the nitrene; (ii) photolysis of the same azide gives nitrene products (C–H insertion, aziridines) besides isocyanate; (iii) the rate of Curtius rearrangement correlates with migratory aptitude of R and is accelerated by electron-releasing R, which implies migration is concerted with N<sub>2</sub> loss; (iv) migration occurs with retention of configuration at the migrating carbon (Wallis and Nagel, 1931: optically active 2-methyl-2-phenylbutanamide → optically active amine); (v) crossover experiments show no free acylnitrene.",
          "<b>Migratory aptitude.</b> tertiary alkyl &gt; cyclohexyl ≈ secondary alkyl ≈ benzyl ≈ aryl &gt; primary alkyl &gt; methyl (electron-rich groups migrate best). Mixed alkyl–aryl behaviour depends on conditions: in Hofmann, p-methoxy aryl &gt; aryl; ⚑ for JEE state: ‘the group attached to C=O migrates’ and ignore competition; there is only one migrating group in an amide RCONH<sub>2</sub>.",
          "<b>The carbon-count rule.</b> RCONH<sub>2</sub> → RNH<sub>2</sub>; for the dicarboxylic diamide H<sub>2</sub>NCO–(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>n</sub>–CONH<sub>2</sub> the product is H<sub>2</sub>N–(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>n</sub>–NH<sub>2</sub> (two carbons less in total); for phthalimide → anthranilic acid (o-aminobenzoic acid) (industrial route to anthranilate esters and indigo, 4 NaOH + Br<sub>2</sub>/NaOCl); for succinimide → β-alanine; for phthalamide → o-phenylenediamine… (with 2 Hofmann steps).",
          "<b>The Hofmann elimination is a different reaction</b> (E2 of a quaternary ammonium hydroxide, giving the least substituted alkene). Do not confuse ‘Hofmann bromamide degradation’ and ‘Hofmann exhaustive methylation/elimination’.",
          "<b>Industrial and synthetic uses.</b> Curtius: biologically active carbamates (Boc/Cbz amines from acids), the Tamiflu synthesis; Hofmann: production of anthranilic acid; Schmidt: caprolactam alternative route (HN<sub>3</sub> is dangerous; Beckmann with H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> is used on the 10<sup>6</sup>-tonne scale); Lossen: amino-acid-derived isocyanates; isocyanates are monomers of polyurethanes (TDI, MDI: made industrially from phosgene, whose accident was the Bhopal MIC release)."])
    B.h2("11.10  Choosing the right rearrangement: a decision table")
    B.table(["Question asks for…", "Think of", "Check"],
        [["amine with one carbon less than the amide", "Hofmann (Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH)", "RCONH<sub>2</sub> must be a primary amide"],
         ["amine from acid chloride via azide", "Curtius", "NaN<sub>3</sub> then Δ then H<sub>2</sub>O"],
         ["carbamate/urethane R–NH–COOR′", "Curtius or Hofmann in the presence of ROH", "isocyanate + R′OH"],
         ["amide from ketone with HN<sub>3</sub>", "Schmidt", "larger/aryl group migrates more easily"],
         ["amide from oxime", "Beckmann", "anti group migrates; nylon-6 from cyclohexanone oxime"],
         ["aziridine from alkene + sulfonyl azide / iminoiodinane + Cu", "nitrene transfer", "stereochemistry retained for singlet"],
         ["carbazole from 2-nitrobiphenyl + P(OEt)<sub>3</sub>", "Cadogan reductive cyclisation", "aryl nitrene inserts into the neighbouring C–H"],
         ["N-chloramine + hν, H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> → pyrrolidine", "Hofmann–Löffler–Freytag", "radical 1,5-HAT, not nitrene (Part 9)"],
         ["p-aminophenol from N-phenylhydroxylamine", "Bamberger rearrangement", "nitrenium ion"]],
        widths=[36, 30, 34])
    B.warn("<b>Typical errors in nitrene/rearrangement questions.</b> (1) Writing the Hofmann reaction with a tertiary or N-substituted amide – an N–H on the nitrogen is required to form the bromoamide anion. (2) Forgetting that the product amine is formed after loss of CO<sub>2</sub> (as carbonate in alkaline medium), and that the amine has the same R as the amide. (3) Applying Hofmann to a nitrile or an ester. (4) Believing the migrating group in Beckmann is the larger group: it is the one <i>anti</i> to the leaving group. (5) Treating the Curtius as proceeding through a free nitrene. (6) Confusing nitrene (neutral, six electrons, monovalent) with nitrile oxide/azide/amide anion. (7) Writing Hofmann bromamide reaction for alcohol/oxime. (8) Forgetting that aryl azide photolysis in the presence of amine gives azepines, not anilines.")


def mechanisms(B):
    c = []
    c.append(ex("11.6", "Lossen rearrangement: O-acylhydroxamate anion → isocyanate",
        "[CH3:1][C:2](=[O:3])[N-:4][O:5][C:6](=[O:7])[CH3:8]",
        [St([A(('b', 1, 2), ('nb', 1, 4), 0.5), A(('b', 4, 5), ('lp', 5), -0.5), A(('lp', 4), ('nb', 4, 2), 0.5)], "−CH_3COO^−", expect="CN=C=O.CC(=O)[O-]")],
        "CN=C=O.CC(=O)[O-]",
        "After deprotonation of the N–H (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 8–9), the methyl group migrates from carbonyl carbon to nitrogen as carboxylate leaves, exactly as bromide leaves in the Hofmann reaction (11.4) and N<sub>2</sub> in the Curtius reaction (11.3). The leaving-group ability (N<sub>2</sub> &gt; carboxylate ≈ Br<sup>−</sup> in the anion) controls the temperature required: Curtius 60–100 °C, Lossen 25–100 °C with base.", width=680))
    c.append(ex("11.7", "Isocyanate trapped by methanol: carbamate (urethane) formation",
        "[CH3:1][N:2]=[C:3]=[O:4].[CH3:10][O:11][H:12]",
        [St([A(('lp', 11), ('nb', 11, 3), 0.5), A(('b', 2, 3), ('lp', 2), 0.5)], "ROH adds to C=N", expect="C[N-]C(=O)[O+]([H])C".replace("C[N-]C(=O)[O+]([H])C", "C[N-]C(=O)[OH+]C")),
         St([A(('lp', 2), ('nb', 2, 12), 0.5), A(('b', 11, 12), ('lp', 11), -0.5)], "proton shift", expect="CNC(=O)OC")],
        "CNC(=O)OC",
        "The alcohol oxygen attacks the electrophilic isocyanate carbon (the C=N π bond is the more reactive, giving an amidate anion), and a proton transfer yields the carbamate CH<sub>3</sub>NHCO<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>. With water the carbamic acid decarboxylates to the amine; with an amine the product is a urea. The same chemistry is the basis of polyurethane and polyurea manufacture.", width=680))
    c.append(ex("11.8", "Alkylnitrene → imine (1,2-hydride shift)",
        "[CH3:1][CH:2]([H:4])[N:3]",
        [St([A(('b', 2, 4), ('nb', 3, 4), 0.5), A(('lp', 3), ('nb', 2, 3), -0.5)], "1,2-H shift", charges={3: 0}, expect="CC=N")],
        "CC=N",
        "The C–H bond pair migrates to the electron-deficient nitrogen and a lone pair of nitrogen forms the C=N π bond. The shift is very fast (&gt; 10<sup>10</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>) for singlet alkylnitrenes, which is why alkyl azides photolysed at low temperatures give imines rather than insertion products.", width=680))
    c.append(ex("11.9", "Beckmann rearrangement: anti-methyl migrates in protonated acetone oxime",
        "[CH3:1][C:2]([CH3:7])=[N:3][OH2+:4]",
        [St([A(('b', 1, 2), ('nb', 1, 3), 0.5), A(('b', 3, 4), ('lp', 4), -0.5)], "anti-migration, −H_2O", expect="C[C+]=NC.O"),
         St(A(('lp', 20), ('nb', 20, 2), 0.5), "water traps the nitrilium ion", add="[OH2:20]", expect="CC(=NC)[OH2+].O")],
        "CC(=NC)[OH2+].O",
        "Protonation converts OH into the leaving group H<sub>2</sub>O. As water leaves, the group <i>anti</i> to it (here a methyl) migrates from carbon to nitrogen, forming the nitrilium ion CH<sub>3</sub>–C<sup>+</sup>=N–CH<sub>3</sub> ↔ CH<sub>3</sub>–C≡N<sup>+</sup>–CH<sub>3</sub>. Water adds to carbon, and after a proton transfer the amide N-methylacetamide results. Contrast the Hofmann/Curtius: there the migrating group is on the carbonyl carbon and the leaving group is on the same nitrogen; here the migrating group is the one in the anti position on the C=N.", width=680))
    B.grid(c, 2)


def problems(B, q):
    q(11, "Give the product of Hofmann bromamide degradation of (a) CH<sub>3</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>, (b) (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CCONH<sub>2</sub>, (c) C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>, (d) cyclohexanecarboxamide, (e) H<sub>2</sub>NCO(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>4</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>.",
      S([("a", "CN"), ("b", "CC(C)(C)N"), ("c", "NCc1ccccc1"), ("d", "NC1CCCCC1"), ("e", "NCCCCN")]), "(a) CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>; (b) t-BuNH<sub>2</sub>; (c) benzylamine; (d) cyclohexylamine; (e) 1,4-diaminobutane (putrescine)", "R–CONH<sub>2</sub> → R–NH<sub>2</sub>; for the diamide both CONH<sub>2</sub> groups are degraded, leaving H<sub>2</sub>N–(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>4</sub>–NH<sub>2</sub> (adipamide has 6 C: two are lost as carbonate).")
    q(12, "Phthalimide is treated with Br<sub>2</sub> and excess aqueous NaOH. Identify the product after acidification, and state its use.",
      S([("", "O=C1NC(=O)c2ccccc12"), ("→", "Nc1ccccc1C(=O)O")]), "anthranilic acid (2-aminobenzoic acid)", "NaOH opens the imide to the phthalamate; the remaining CONH<sub>2</sub> undergoes Hofmann degradation to give NH<sub>2</sub> on the ring while the carboxylate stays. Anthranilic acid is the starting material for indigo, methyl anthranilate (perfumery) and benzyne generation (via diazotisation to benzenediazonium-2-carboxylate).")
    q(13, "Convert (a) CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COOH to CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> by two methods, (b) CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COOH to CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>.",
      None, "(a) (i) SOCl<sub>2</sub>; NaN<sub>3</sub>; Δ; H<sub>2</sub>O (Curtius), or (ii) SOCl<sub>2</sub>; NH<sub>3</sub>; Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH (Hofmann); (b) reduce acid or its amide to the alcohol/amine: CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COOH → CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub> → LiAlH<sub>4</sub> → CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>", "Hofmann/Curtius remove one carbon; to preserve the carbon count use LiAlH<sub>4</sub> reduction of the amide (or nitrile). To add one carbon: acid → alcohol → bromide → CN<sup>−</sup> → LiAlH<sub>4</sub>.")
    q(14, "An optically active amide, (R)-2-phenylbutanamide, is subjected to Hofmann degradation. State the product, its configuration and why optical purity is retained.",
      None, "(R)-1-phenylpropylamine (retention); no racemisation", "The migrating carbon never becomes a free cation or radical; it is bound to both C and N in the three-centre transition state throughout, so its configuration is retained (the change of CIP descriptor depends only on priority changes: here NH<sub>2</sub> &gt; Ph &gt; Et, so the descriptor remains R).")
    q(15, "Which of the following can undergo Hofmann bromamide degradation? (a) CH<sub>3</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>, (b) CH<sub>3</sub>CONHCH<sub>3</sub>, (c) CH<sub>3</sub>CON(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>, (d) CH<sub>3</sub>COOC<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>, (e) CH<sub>3</sub>CN, (f) HCONH<sub>2</sub>.",
      None, "(a) only (and arguably (f) gives NH<sub>3</sub>/HNCO but not a meaningful amine)", "Only primary amides RCONH<sub>2</sub> have two N–H bonds: one is replaced by Br, the other deprotonated to the bromoamide anion that rearranges. N-Substituted amides lack the second N–H, and (d), (e) have no N–H at all.")
    q(16, "Name the product of Schmidt reaction of (a) cyclohexanone + HN<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, (b) acetophenone + HN<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, (c) benzoic acid + HN<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>.",
      S([("a", "O=C1CCCCCN1"), ("b", "CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1"), ("c", "Nc1ccccc1")]), "(a) ε-caprolactam; (b) acetanilide (phenyl migrates); (c) aniline", "(a) ring expansion: the ring carbon migrates to nitrogen of the iminodiazonium ion. (b) the group that migrates is the one anti to N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> in the preferred geometry; with aryl alkyl ketones the aryl group migrates to give an N-aryl amide. (c) acid → amine with one carbon less.")
    q(17, "Compare the Beckmann rearrangement of cyclohexanone oxime with the Schmidt reaction of cyclohexanone. What do they have in common, and which is used industrially for nylon-6?",
      None, "same product (ε-caprolactam); Beckmann", "Both produce a seven-membered lactam by ring-carbon migration to nitrogen with loss of a leaving group (H<sub>2</sub>O or N<sub>2</sub>). Industrially the Beckmann (oleum) is used because HN<sub>3</sub> is toxic and explosive. ε-Caprolactam is polymerised (ring-opening, 250 °C) to nylon-6.")
    q(18, "In the Beckmann rearrangement of the E-oxime of 4-methylacetophenone the group anti to OH is the aryl group. Write the product and predict the product from the Z-oxime.",
      S([("E", "CC(=NO)c1ccc(C)cc1"), ("from E", "CC(=O)Nc1ccc(C)cc1"), ("from Z", "Cc1ccc(C(=O)NC)cc1")]), "E: N-(4-methylphenyl)acetamide (aryl migrates); Z: N-methyl-4-methylbenzamide", "The migrating group is always the one anti to the leaving group; under acid conditions oximes interconvert and the more stable E isomer predominates, but under non-equilibrating conditions (TsCl/pyridine, PCl<sub>5</sub> at low T) the product reflects the oxime geometry.")
    q(19, "State whether each of the following contains a free nitrene intermediate under the stated conditions: (a) CH<sub>3</sub>CON<sub>3</sub>, 80 °C; (b) CH<sub>3</sub>CON<sub>3</sub>, hν; (c) PhN<sub>3</sub>, hν; (d) TsN=IPh + Cu(I); (e) o-nitrobiphenyl + P(OEt)<sub>3</sub>.",
      None, "(a) no (concerted Curtius); (b) partly yes; (c) yes (singlet then triplet); (d) no (copper–imido, nitrenoid); (e) yes (arylnitrene, or nitrenoid with phosphorus)", "Thermal Curtius gives isocyanate cleanly; photolysis yields insertion/addition products indicating real acylnitrene; phenylnitrene is formed from PhN<sub>3</sub> and ring-expands; Cu and Rh give metal-bound nitrene, and Cadogan intermediates are nitrene-like species whose cyclisation is on the nitrene or its deoxygenation precursor (nitroso).")
    q(20, "Phenyl azide is heated in benzene and the product is mainly azobenzene together with aniline; when irradiated at −196 °C in a glass, an ESR signal appears. Identify the intermediate.",
      None, "triplet phenylnitrene", "The ESR signal (zero-field splitting D ≈ 1 cm<sup>−1</sup>) is the fingerprint of a triplet (S = 1). Dimerisation of two triplet nitrenes gives PhN=NPh; H-abstraction from solvent gives PhNH<sup>•</sup> and PhNH<sub>2</sub>.")
    q(21, "(Match the column) Reaction – leaving group lost from nitrogen: (A) Hofmann, (B) Curtius, (C) Lossen, (D) Beckmann (P) N<sub>2</sub>, (Q) Br<sup>−</sup>, (R) RCOO<sup>−</sup>, (S) H<sub>2</sub>O.",
      None, "A–Q; B–P; C–R; D–S", "Hofmann: N-bromoamide anion → bromide; Curtius: acyl azide → N<sub>2</sub>; Lossen: O-acyl hydroxamate → carboxylate; Beckmann: protonated oxime → water.")
    q(22, "A compound A (C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>11</sub>NO) is a primary amide. On Hofmann degradation it gives an optically active amine B (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>11</sub>N). Identify A and B.",
      S([("A", "CCC(C)C(N)=O"), ("B", "CCC(C)N")]), "A = 2-methylbutanamide; B = butan-2-amine", "Of the four C<sub>5</sub> primary amides only 2-methylbutanamide gives a chiral amine, since the migrating sec-butyl carbon retains configuration. The others give n-butyl, isobutyl and tert-butylamines, all achiral.")
    q(23, "Explain why bromamide anion RCON<sup>−</sup>Br rearranges at 60–80 °C but N-chloroamides or N-bromoamides are stable at 0 °C.",
      None, "the amide N–H must be removed to give the anion; the neutral N-bromoamide is stable", "Rearrangement requires the negatively charged nitrogen whose lone pair forms the new C=N π bond as the migrating group moves; in the neutral N-haloamide that lone pair is delocalised into C=O. Only with base is the nitrogen deprotonated and the isocyanate-forming step possible.")
    q(24, "Predict the product: 2-nitrobiphenyl + triethyl phosphite, 160 °C.",
      S([("", "O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1-c1ccccc1"), ("→", "c1ccc2c(c1)[nH]c1ccccc12")]), "carbazole", "P(OEt)<sub>3</sub> deoxygenates the nitro group twice (to the nitroso then nitrene stage; 2 equiv of phosphite give 2 O=P(OEt)<sub>3</sub>); the aryl nitrene then inserts into the adjacent ring C–H bond (electrocyclisation/1,5-insertion) to form the five-membered ring (Cadogan).")
    q(25, "N-aminophthalimide is oxidised with Pb(OAc)<sub>4</sub> in the presence of cis-2-butene and trans-2-butene separately. Predict the stereochemistry.",
      None, "cis → cis-aziridine; trans → trans-aziridine", "N-Phthalimidonitrene is a singlet aminonitrene; singlets add in a single concerted syn step, so alkene stereochemistry is retained (Atkinson).")
    q(26, "Which is more stable, a triplet or singlet phenylnitrene, and why does phenyl azide photolysis in diethylamine give an azepine rather than N,N-diethyl-N′-phenylhydrazine?",
      None, "triplet; azepine arises from singlet-derived ring expansion", "Triplet PhN: is the ground state, but the initially formed singlet is converted (k ≈ 10<sup>9</sup>–10<sup>10</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>) to benzazirine then didehydroazepine before it can intersystem-cross; the strained cyclic ketenimine reacts with nucleophiles (Et<sub>2</sub>NH adds) to give 2-diethylamino-3H-azepine. With ortho substituents or at low T the ISC pathway wins.")
    q(27, "Distinguish nitrene, nitrenium ion, nitrenoid and nitrile by (i) electron count, (ii) charge, (iii) typical reactivity.",
      None, "see reasoning", "Nitrene R–N: (6 e, neutral, electrophilic/insertion/addition); nitrenium R<sub>2</sub>N<sup>+</sup> (6 e, +, electrophile; attacks aromatic ring or DNA bases); nitrenoid M=NR or X–NR<sup>−</sup> (transfer of NR, with metal/halide still attached); nitrile R–C≡N (8 e, stable).")
    q(28, "In the Hofmann degradation of the amide PhCH<sub>2</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub> with Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH in methanol (instead of water) the product is a carbamate. Give its structure and explain.",
      S([("", "COC(=O)NCc1ccccc1")]), "methyl N-benzylcarbamate", "The isocyanate PhCH<sub>2</sub>N=C=O is trapped by the solvent methanol (faster than by hydroxide). This ‘modified Hofmann’ is used to obtain protected amines; the carbamate can be hydrolysed or hydrogenolysed to the amine.")
    q(29, "How many different primary amines C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>11</sub>N can be obtained by Hofmann degradation of primary amides C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>11</sub>NO?",
      None, "4", "The four pentanamide skeletons (pentanamide, 2-methylbutanamide, 3-methylbutanamide, 2,2-dimethylpropanamide) give n-butylamine, sec-butylamine, isobutylamine and tert-butylamine – the four primary C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>11</sub>N amines.")
    q(30, "Why do aryl azides decompose at lower temperatures than alkyl azides, and why do aryl nitrenes ring-expand while alkyl nitrenes undergo 1,2-H shifts?",
      None, "conjugation with the arene lowers the TS energy; the aryl π system offers cyclisation, the alkyl group offers a migrating H", "Conjugation of the azide with the ring stabilises the transition state for N<sub>2</sub> loss (aryl azides lose N<sub>2</sub> at ≈ 100–140 °C, alkyl azides at higher temperatures or by photolysis). The singlet arylnitrene can attack the ortho carbon (benzazirine → didehydroazepine); an alkylnitrene has no π system and the adjacent C–H bond migrates to nitrogen to give an imine.")
